<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mbegan Ndour</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1494</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mbegan Ndour was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> kingdom of Saloum in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, around 1494. A warrior linked through his mother to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelowar">Guelowar</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Kingdom of Sine</a>, he is said in tradition to have killed Eli <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banabhatta">Bana</a>, the Muslim cleric who then ruled the region. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahone">Kahone</a> his capital, and Saloum grew rich from the salt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trades</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mbegan Ndour was born to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> father from Saloum and a Serer princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a>, and through his mother belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelowar">Guelowar</a> maternal dynasty that ruled Sine and Saloum for over six hundred years. In the tradition of Sine his father was Maari Nduur of Mboudaye, nicknamed Marga Tiatj, a hunter and healer still remembered in Sine, Saloum and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a>, and his mother was the princess Nyillane Faye, sister of the Maad a Sinig Diessanou Faye. A legend of Saloum makes him a nephew of Maad a Sinig Maissa Wali instead, probably a later invention to legitimate him, since the timelines do not match.</p><p>Tradition tells that the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a> at first refused to let his sister marry Maari, a man of low status, and would not host the naming ceremony of her son. In the story Maari drew an animal to the ceremony for the sacrifice by his powers and kept the guests dry inside a circle during heavy rain, after which the king accepted the marriage and the child.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His early career is the subject of numerous, sometimes contradictory oral traditions. Most likely he fought in wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baol">Baol</a>, won a reputation as a general and may have ruled for a time as Teigne of Baol. He allied himself with the Buurba Jolof Biram Njeme Kumba, and the support of the Ndiaye dynasty was important in the founding of Saloum. As a potential rival for power he was not welcome at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a>, and was sent to his father's homeland to carve out a kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> of Mbey was under the influence of Eli Bana, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toucouleur">Toucouleur</a> marabout whose attempts to convert the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> had caused friction, so that many joined Mbegan Ndour, whose forces were also strengthened with horses and guns bought from European merchants on the coast. In the legend he became a snake and bit the marabout outside his mosque. He then defeated Diattara Tambedou, a Muslim Soninke and probably a salt merchant: control of salt underpinned the conflict as much as religion. Ruling as Maad Saloum, he allied with Serer lamanes, marabouts and village chiefs, though he directly controlled only Mbey, and later he may have served as regent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a> for an underage king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mbegane Ndour” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saloum was founded around 1494 by <b>Mbegan Ndour</b>, a Serer warrior linked through his mother to the Guelowar of the Kingdom of Sine, who according to tradition killed Eli Bana, the Muslim cleric then ruling the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-saloum">Kingdom of Saloum</a></li></ul></section>
